        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: Let's denote the common ratio of the geometric sequence by \\( r \\) and the common difference of the arithmetic sequence by \\( d \\). For any positive integer \\( k \\), we have:\n\\[ a_{2k} = a_{2k-1}r \\]\n\\[ a_{2k+1} = a_{2k-1}r^2 \\]\n\\[ a_{2k+1} = a_{2k} + d = a_{2k-1}r + d \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 429,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 430,
        "char_start": 282,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 183,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 184,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: From the above, we can express \\( d \\) in terms of \\( a_{2k-1} \\) and \\( r \\):\n\\[ a_{2k-1}r^2 = a_{2k-1}r + d \\]\n\\[ d = a_{2k-1}(r^2 - r) \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 613,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 614,
        "char_start": 432,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 287,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 288,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Now, consider the next term in the sequence:\n\\[ a_{2k+2} = a_{2k+1} + d = a_{2k-1}r^2 + a_{2k-1}(r^2 - r) = a_{2k-1}r^2(1 + 1 - \\frac{1}{r}) = a_{2k-1}r^2(2 - \\frac{1}{r}) \\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 976,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 977,
        "char_start": 616,
        "effective_step": 4,
        "end_token_index": 480,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 481,
        "step_number": 4,
        "text": "Step 4: Since \\( a_{2k+2} \\) must also be part of a geometric sequence starting from \\( a_{2k+1} \\), we have:\n\\[ a_{2k+2} = a_{2k+1}r = a_{2k-1}r^2r = a_{2k-1}r^3 \\]\nEquating the two expressions for \\( a_{2k+2} \\):\n\\[ a_{2k-1}r^2(2 - \\frac{1}{r}) = a_{2k-1}r^3 \\]\n\\[ 2 - \\frac{1}{r} = r \\]\n\\[ 2r - 1 = r^2 \\]\n\\[ r^2 - 2r + 1 = 0 \\]\n\\[ (r-1)^2 = 0 \\]\n\\[ r = 1 \\]"
      },
